    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Viktor says he needs to work. Lanovsky starts monologuing about how people call him a scary person because he hunts people without documents. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, давайте только быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я перейду сразу к делу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы слышали что-нибудь обо мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, слышали во всех странах.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Знаете, как меня называют?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Меня называют страшным человеком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Месяц назад была проверка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они ничего тут не нашли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мятную конфету не желаете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне не нужно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Наша служба похожа на птиц.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мы всегда смотрим на вас сверху.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, давайте только быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, let us just do it quickly.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'good' with 'quickly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я перейду сразу к делу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will get straight to the point.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'case'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы слышали что-нибудь обо мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Have you heard anything about me?' The character combines 'you' with 'to me' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, слышали во всех странах.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, heard in all countries.' Chef Viktor links 'yes' directly with 'did you hear?' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Знаете, как меня называют?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you know what they call me?' The character combines 'know' with 'call' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Меня называют страшным человеком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They call me a scary person.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'me' with 'person'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Месяц назад была проверка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A month ago there was an inspection.' The '-a' ending on the past-tense verb for 'was' shows that the subject is feminine.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они ничего тут не нашли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They did not find anything here.' Here the verb for 'found' takes the plural past-tense ending '-li', which is also used for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мятную конфету не желаете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want a mint candy?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'would you like'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, мне не нужно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I do not need it.' Chef Viktor combines 'no' with 'necessary' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Наша служба похожа на птиц.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Our service is like birds.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'our' with 'birds'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мы всегда смотрим на вас сверху.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We always look at you from above.' The character uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'upstairs', keeping a formal or respectful register. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
